{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000109",
  "title": "Wave Equation",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[dependencies]\nplotters = \"0.3\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": null,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# 1D Wave Equation 🌊\n\nThe **wave equation** is one of the fundamental PDEs in physics:\n\n> **∂²u/∂t² = c² · ∂²u/∂x²**\n\nIt describes how disturbances propagate through a medium — vibrating strings, sound waves, electromagnetic radiation, and even quantum mechanical wavefunctions.\n\n## Finite Difference Method\n\nWe discretize space and time into a grid, approximating derivatives with differences:\n\n- **Space**: N = 200 points, dx = 0.01\n- **Time**: dt = 0.005, c = 1.0\n- **CFL condition**: c·dt/dx = 0.5 ≤ 1 ✓ (ensures stability)\n- **Boundary**: Fixed ends u(0) = u(L) = 0\n- **Initial condition**: Gaussian pulse centered at x = 0.3\n\nThe update rule is:\n\n> u(i, n+1) = 2·u(i, n) - u(i, n-1) + r²·[u(i+1, n) - 2·u(i, n) + u(i-1, n)]\n\nwhere r = c·dt/dx is the **Courant number**.\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Wave Simulation",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "// 1D wave equation solver using the finite difference method\n\nlet nx = 200_usize;      // Spatial grid points\nlet c = 1.0_f64;         // Wave speed\nlet dx = 0.01_f64;       // Spatial step\nlet dt = 0.005_f64;      // Time step\nlet total_steps = 500_usize;\nlet snapshot_interval = 50_usize;\n\n// Courant number (must be <= 1 for stability)\nlet r = c * dt / dx;\nlet r2 = r * r;\n\n// Spatial coordinates\nlet x: Vec<f64> = (0..nx).map(|i| i as f64 * dx).collect();\nlet x_center = 0.3 * (nx as f64 * dx);\nlet sigma = 0.05_f64;\n\n// Initial condition: Gaussian pulse\nlet mut u_prev: Vec<f64> = x.iter()\n    .map(|&xi| (-(xi - x_center).powi(2) / (2.0 * sigma * sigma)).exp())\n    .collect();\n\n// First time step (using u_t(x,0) = 0 initial velocity condition)\nlet mut u_curr: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; nx];\nfor i in 1..(nx - 1) {\n    u_curr[i] = u_prev[i]\n        + 0.5 * r2 * (u_prev[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_prev[i] + u_prev[i - 1]);\n}\n// Fixed boundary conditions\nu_curr[0] = 0.0;\nu_curr[nx - 1] = 0.0;\n\n// Store snapshots for visualization\nlet mut snapshots: Vec<Vec<f64>> = vec![u_prev.clone()];\nif snapshot_interval <= 1 {\n    snapshots.push(u_curr.clone());\n}\n\n// Time-stepping loop\nfor step in 2..=total_steps {\n    let mut u_next = vec![0.0; nx];\n    for i in 1..(nx - 1) {\n        u_next[i] = 2.0 * u_curr[i] - u_prev[i]\n            + r2 * (u_curr[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_curr[i] + u_curr[i - 1]);\n    }\n    // Fixed boundaries\n    u_next[0] = 0.0;\n    u_next[nx - 1] = 0.0;\n\n    u_prev = u_curr;\n    u_curr = u_next;\n\n    // Save snapshot at regular intervals\n    if step % snapshot_interval == 0 {\n        snapshots.push(u_curr.clone());\n    }\n}\n\nCellOutput::text(format!(\n    \"Wave simulation: {} spatial points, {} steps, {} snapshots captured.\\nCourant number r = {:.2}\",\n    nx, total_steps, snapshots.len(), r\n))\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Wave Propagation Visualization",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use plotters::prelude::*;\n\n// Re-run the simulation for full data\nlet nx = 200_usize;\nlet c = 1.0_f64;\nlet dx = 0.01_f64;\nlet dt = 0.005_f64;\nlet total_steps = 500_usize;\nlet snapshot_interval = 50_usize;\n\nlet r = c * dt / dx;\nlet r2 = r * r;\n\nlet x: Vec<f64> = (0..nx).map(|i| i as f64 * dx).collect();\nlet x_center = 0.3 * (nx as f64 * dx);\nlet sigma = 0.05_f64;\n\nlet mut u_prev: Vec<f64> = x.iter()\n    .map(|&xi| (-(xi - x_center).powi(2) / (2.0 * sigma * sigma)).exp())\n    .collect();\nlet mut u_curr: Vec<f64> = vec![0.0; nx];\nfor i in 1..(nx - 1) {\n    u_curr[i] = u_prev[i]\n        + 0.5 * r2 * (u_prev[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_prev[i] + u_prev[i - 1]);\n}\nu_curr[0] = 0.0;\nu_curr[nx - 1] = 0.0;\n\nlet mut snapshots: Vec<Vec<f64>> = vec![u_prev.clone()];\n\nfor step in 2..=total_steps {\n    let mut u_next = vec![0.0; nx];\n    for i in 1..(nx - 1) {\n        u_next[i] = 2.0 * u_curr[i] - u_prev[i]\n            + r2 * (u_curr[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_curr[i] + u_curr[i - 1]);\n    }\n    u_next[0] = 0.0;\n    u_next[nx - 1] = 0.0;\n    u_prev = u_curr;\n    u_curr = u_next;\n    if step % snapshot_interval == 0 {\n        snapshots.push(u_curr.clone());\n    }\n}\n\nlet n_snaps = snapshots.len();\n\n// Color palette: cycle through distinct colors for each time snapshot\nlet palette = [\n    RGBColor(255, 255, 100), // t=0: yellow (initial pulse)\n    RGBColor(100, 220, 255), // cyan\n    RGBColor(100, 255, 140), // green\n    RGBColor(255, 160, 80),  // orange\n    RGBColor(200, 130, 255), // purple\n    RGBColor(255, 100, 130), // pink\n    RGBColor(130, 200, 255), // light blue\n    RGBColor(255, 220, 100), // gold\n    RGBColor(100, 255, 220), // teal\n    RGBColor(255, 130, 180), // rose\n    RGBColor(180, 255, 100), // lime\n];\n\nlet x_max = (nx - 1) as f64 * dx;\n\nlet mut svg_buf = String::new();\n{\n    let root = SVGBackend::with_string(&mut svg_buf, (800, 500))\n        .into_drawing_area();\n    root.fill(&RGBColor(15, 15, 25)).unwrap();\n\n    let mut chart = ChartBuilder::on(&root)\n        .caption(\"Wave Propagation Over Time\", (\"sans-serif\", 22).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .margin(15)\n        .x_label_area_size(35)\n        .y_label_area_size(50)\n        .build_cartesian_2d(0.0_f64..x_max, -1.2_f64..1.2)\n        .unwrap();\n\n    chart\n        .configure_mesh()\n        .x_desc(\"Position x\")\n        .y_desc(\"Displacement u(x, t)\")\n        .label_style((\"sans-serif\", 13).into_font().color(&RGBColor(180, 180, 180)))\n        .axis_style(RGBColor(60, 60, 80))\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    // Draw each time snapshot as a line\n    for (s_idx, snap) in snapshots.iter().enumerate() {\n        let color = palette[s_idx % palette.len()];\n        let time = if s_idx == 0 {\n            0.0\n        } else {\n            s_idx as f64 * snapshot_interval as f64 * dt\n        };\n\n        let data: Vec<(f64, f64)> = x.iter()\n            .zip(snap.iter())\n            .map(|(&xi, &ui)| (xi, ui))\n            .collect();\n\n        chart.draw_series(LineSeries::new(data.into_iter(), color))\n            .unwrap()\n            .label(format!(\"t = {:.2}s\", time))\n            .legend(move |(lx, ly)| {\n                Rectangle::new([(lx, ly - 3), (lx + 10, ly + 3)], color.filled())\n            });\n    }\n\n    chart\n        .configure_series_labels()\n        .background_style(RGBColor(30, 30, 45).mix(0.9))\n        .label_font((\"sans-serif\", 11).into_font().color(&WHITE))\n        .border_style(RGBColor(80, 80, 100))\n        .position(SeriesLabelPosition::UpperRight)\n        .draw()\n        .unwrap();\n\n    root.present().unwrap();\n}\n\nSvg(svg_buf)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_3",
      "order": 3,
      "label": "Wave Physics",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Understanding the Results\n\n### Reflection at Boundaries\n\nThe Gaussian pulse splits into two halves traveling in opposite directions. When a wave reaches a **fixed boundary** (u = 0), it reflects with **inverted phase** — the pulse flips upside down. This is because the fixed endpoint must remain at zero displacement.\n\n### Superposition\n\nWhen the reflected waves pass through each other, they **superpose** — their displacements add linearly. This is a direct consequence of the wave equation being **linear**: any sum of solutions is also a solution.\n\n### Standing Waves\n\nAfter multiple reflections, the overlapping traveling waves create **standing wave patterns** — certain points (nodes) remain stationary while others (antinodes) oscillate with maximum amplitude. The fundamental frequency of a string of length L and wave speed c is:\n\n> **f₁ = c / (2L)**\n\n### Conservation\n\nThe finite difference scheme we used is **energy-conserving** (when the CFL condition is satisfied). The total wave energy:\n\n> **E = ½∫(u_t² + c²u_x²)dx**\n\nremains constant throughout the simulation — no artificial damping or amplification.\n\n### Try Modifying\n\n- Change the initial pulse position or width\n- Increase the wave speed c\n- Add a second pulse and watch interference patterns\n- Try different boundary conditions (free ends: du/dx = 0)\n",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ]
}
